  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Aerovironment has received $511M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Aerovironment won 67 awards this period, with an average award size of $7.6M, and obligations are up 3.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the primary buyer, obligating $510M. The Department of Transportation and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration are minor buyers, obligating $575K and $111K respectively. Aircraft Manufacturing is the dominant market for Aerovironment federal work, accounting for $489M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is a smaller market at $21.9M. The historical obligations series shows growth and variability: FY21 was $10.4M, FY22 was $58.9M, FY23 was $321M, FY24 was $215M, FY25 was $496M, and FY26 is $511M. The note: all figures are contract obligations except award-value figures in largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables, and current-year totals are partial. This summary focuses on obligations, buying agencies, and the top markets driving Aerovironment federal revenue in FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Aerovironment's federal business trended over recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "Aerovironment's federal obligations rose to $511M in FY26 to date, after $496M in FY25 and $215M in FY24. Aerovironment recorded $321M in FY23, $58.9M in FY22, and $10.4M in FY21. Aerovironment's obligations are up 3.0% year over year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and markets drive Aerovironment's federal revenue?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $510M. The Department of Transportation obligates $575K and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligates $111K. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest market at $489M, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $21.9M."
      }
